From: Gemini PDA Linux port <mratan/gemini-linux>
Subject: [PATCH] camera: raw Bayer frames land in memory (#65)

Three defects, each of which alone was enough to deliver nothing, and each
of which was invisible behind the one upstream of it.

1. SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_CTL bit 4 (new name RESYNC_MERGE_EN). Set,
   the D-PHY trains perfectly -- analog bank byte-identical to the
   vendor's, clock lane FSM 0x10, data lanes alternating 0x01011010
   exactly as the vendor's do -- and the CSI-2 decoder decodes nothing at
   all: zero packets, and not one interrupt of any kind. Clear, packets
   appear immediately. Found by poking the register mid-stream and
   bisecting three candidates; it is the only one of them that moves
   anything.

   0x15 came from a C-PHY code path. So did the 0x15 next door in
   SENINF_CSI2_CTRL_TRIO_CON, which the streaming vendor leaves at 0.

2. SENINF_MUX_CTRL_EXT was programmed BEFORE the mux software reset that
   clears it, so SRC_SEL_EXT read back 0 against the vendor's 1 and the
   FIFO mux ignored the stream: MUX_INTSTA 0, no frame size, nothing
   reaching CAMSV -- with a decoder that by then worked. It is now
   programmed after the reset.

   gemini_cam_vendor_regs() then undid it a second time. Its +0x0d2c and
   +0x0d3c values had been transcribed from a diff of SENINF instance 1,
   whose mux is idle on the vendor; the mux that carries video is
   instance 0's, and there the vendor reads 000e0000 and 00000001.

3. seninf_cam_mux was 0, "the identity crossbar, as the vendor uses". The
   vendor does use the identity map -- and consumes the result from
   CAM_A, cam output 0. We consume it from CAMSV0. Swept 0..7 once the
   two faults above were fixed: 2 delivers frames, every other value
   delivers nothing.

Also retires the +0xb10 story that shaped the last several days of this
issue. "The vendor has bits 8..10 set there and we never reach that state"
was a comparison against the vendor's instance 0, whose decoder is idle.
0x71f is that register's reset value; this driver writes 0x1f into it
itself, and the vendor's ACTIVE instance reads 0x1f, exactly as ours does.
There was never a gap. The refuted ORDER knobs are kept as instruments with
their comment corrected, along with the module parameters that had drifted
into the build tree without ever reaching this series.

Measured on build #87 at the machine, 2026-08-26:

  12 start/stop cycles   10/10 frames each, 0 dropped, 0 errors
  sustained capture      200 frames, 200 sof, 0 dropped, 0 errors
  tg frmsize             0x051003cd (1296x972), matching the negotiated
                         format and the mux's own measurement
  sensor TPG, colour bars   4 frames bit-identical, 12 unique values in
                         the frame, 8 bars in the standard order
  optical capture        a recognisable scene; mean 138, std 228, frames
                         differing from one another

---
--- a/drivers/misc/gemini-cam-bridge.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/gemini-cam-bridge.c
@@ -200,6 +200,12 @@
 #define SENINF_CSI2_HS_TRAIL_PARAM		GENMASK(7, 0)
 #define SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_CTL		0x0a74
 #define SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_CPHY_CNT		GENMASK(2, 0)
+/*
+ * Bit 4 gates the CSI-2 decoder behind the resync/merge stage that a C-PHY
+ * link needs and a D-PHY link must not have. Set, the lanes train and nothing
+ * is ever decoded; clear, packets appear. See gemini_cam_rx_config().
+ */
+#define SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_EN		BIT(4)
 #define SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_BYPASS		BIT(10)
 #define SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_CDPHY_SEL		BIT(11)
 #define SENINF_CSI2_MODE			0x0ae8
@@ -375,7 +381,15 @@
 module_param(seninf_mux, uint, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(seninf_mux, "SENINF FIFO mux index (0 = seninf1_mux)");
 
-static unsigned int seninf_cam_mux = 0;	/* identity crossbar, as the vendor uses */
+/*
+ * 2, measured. The vendor's crossbar is the identity map, but the vendor
+ * consumes the stream from CAM_A -- cam output 0 -- and we consume it from
+ * CAMSV0. Swept 0..7 on 2026-08-26 with a working decoder and a working FIFO
+ * mux behind it: 2 delivers frames and SOF interrupts, every other value
+ * delivers nothing at all. Copying the vendor's identity map here was reading
+ * its topology one step too literally.
+ */
+static unsigned int seninf_cam_mux = 2;
 module_param(seninf_cam_mux, uint, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(seninf_cam_mux,
 		 "CAM MUX output index CAMSV0 consumes. CAM0/CAM1 are CAM_A/CAM_B (the ISP), so 2 is the first CAMSV.");
@@ -468,6 +482,39 @@
  * Instance 1 is the vendor's ACTIVE receiver -- it carries the +0x800 page and
  * the offset-calibration values -- which is why seninf_inst defaults to 1.
  */
+/*
+ * Bring-up ORDER experiments (#65). These are kept as instruments, and as a
+ * recorded negative: none of them ever moved anything, singly or combined.
+ *
+ * They were built on a reading of +0xb10 -- "the vendor has bits 8..10 set
+ * there and we never reach that state" -- which was simply wrong. That
+ * comparison was against the vendor's SENINF instance 0, whose decoder is
+ * IDLE; 0x71f is the reset value of a register this driver writes 0x1f into
+ * itself, and the vendor's active instance reads 0x1f exactly as ours does.
+ * Nothing was ever failing to be "arrived at".
+ *
+ * The lesson is narrower than the wasted time: when diffing against a vendor
+ * capture, diff the instance the vendor is actually USING. The real fault was
+ * a configuration value after all -- SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_EN, see
+ * gemini_cam_rx_config().
+ *
+ * phy_late      configure the decoder first, bias the analog afterwards
+ * decoder_reset toggle SENINF_EN after the PHY is up, so the FSM re-latches
+ *               against a settled configuration
+ * hsrx_det      arm HS detection instead of clearing it
+ */
+static bool phy_late;
+module_param(phy_late, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(phy_late, "bias the analog PHY after configuring the decoder");
+
+static bool decoder_reset;
+module_param(decoder_reset, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(decoder_reset, "toggle SENINF_EN once the PHY is up");
+
+static bool hsrx_det;
+module_param(hsrx_det, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(hsrx_det, "leave CSI2 HSRX_DET_EN set instead of clearing it");
+
 static bool vendor_regs = true;
 module_param(vendor_regs, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(vendor_regs,
@@ -1002,10 +1049,25 @@
 	reg_update(inst + SENINF_MIPI_RX_CONBC, 0x030f7f0fu, 0x00050400u);
 	reg_update(inst + SENINF_MIPI_RX_COND0, 0x0000003fu, 0x0000000fu);
 
-	/* Decode configuration the upstream drivers leave at zero. */
-	writel(0x15, inst + SENINF_CSI2_CTRL_TRIO_CON);
+	/*
+	 * Decode configuration the upstream drivers leave at zero. These are
+	 * the streaming vendor's own values, and two of the three used to be
+	 * 0x15 here -- a number carried over from a C-PHY code path.
+	 *
+	 * SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_EN in particular is the whole of #65. With
+	 * it set the receiver behaves perfectly at every level this driver can
+	 * observe -- the analog bank matches the vendor byte for byte, the
+	 * clock lane FSM reads 0x10 and the data lanes alternate 0x01011010,
+	 * exactly as the vendor's do -- and the decoder still counts ZERO
+	 * packets and raises not one interrupt. Clearing it, with nothing else
+	 * changed, starts the packet counter immediately. Measured by poking
+	 * this one register mid-stream, 2026-08-26; the bit is the difference
+	 * between a link that trains and a link that decodes.
+	 */
+	writel(0x00, inst + SENINF_CSI2_CTRL_TRIO_CON);
 	writel(0x402, inst + SENINF_CSI2_MODE);
-	reg_update(inst + SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_CTL, 0x17u, 0x15u);
+	reg_update(inst + SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_CTL,
+		   0x17u | SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_EN, 0x01u);
 	reg_update(inst + SENINF_CSI2_SYNC_RESYNC_CTL, 0x7u, 0x1u);
 	writel(readl(inst + SENINF_CSI2_POST_DETECT_CTL) | 2,
 	       inst + SENINF_CSI2_POST_DETECT_CTL);
@@ -1027,6 +1089,17 @@
 static void gemini_cam_vendor_regs(struct gemini_cam *cam, void __iomem *inst,
 				   void __iomem *mux)
 {
+	/*
+	 * These three were missing from this list until 2026-08-26, because
+	 * the diff they were transcribed from was of the wrong SENINF
+	 * instance. +0x0a74 is the one that mattered: see
+	 * gemini_cam_rx_config(). They are written before CSI2_CTL so the
+	 * decoder is enabled last, against a settled configuration.
+	 */
+	writel(0x00000000, inst + SENINF_CSI2_DPCM);		/* ours 00000080 */
+	writel(0x00000001, inst + SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_CTL);/* ours 00000015 */
+	writel(0x00000000, inst + SENINF_CSI2_CTRL_TRIO_CON);	/* ours 00000015 */
+
 	/* CSI-2 decoder. ours 03896173 -> vendor 0d8961f3 */
 	writel(0x0d8961f3, inst + SENINF_CSI2_CTL);
 	writel(0x00000400, inst + 0x0a08);	/* ours 00001500 */
@@ -1069,8 +1142,16 @@
 	writel(0xa6df8080, mux + SENINF_MUX_CTRL);
 	if (mux != inst)
 		writel(0x86df0080, inst + SENINF_MUX_CTRL);
-	writel(0x000e2000, mux + 0x0d2c);	/* ours 000c2000 */
-	writel(0x00000000, mux + 0x0d3c);	/* ours 00000001 */
+	/*
+	 * These two are per-mux, and until 2026-08-26 they carried INSTANCE
+	 * 1's values -- transcribed from a diff of the receiver instance,
+	 * whose mux is idle on the vendor. On the mux that actually carries
+	 * video, instance 0, the vendor reads 000e0000 and 00000001. Writing
+	 * instance 1's 00000000 to +0x0d3c here silently undid SRC_SEL_EXT
+	 * every time seninf_start() set it, which is the second half of #65.
+	 */
+	writel(0x000e0000, mux + 0x0d2c);
+	writel(0x00000001, mux + 0x0d3c);
 
 	dev_dbg(cam->dev, "vendor register set applied\n");
 }
@@ -1099,7 +1180,8 @@
 	 * because the part predates them. The analog PHY is configured through
 	 * mipi_RX_ana instead, and the SENINF-to-PHY mapping is fixed.
 	 */
-	gemini_cam_phy_setup(cam);
+	if (!phy_late)
+		gemini_cam_phy_setup(cam);
 
 	reg_update(inst + SENINF_MIPI_RX_CON24,
 		   SENINF_MIPI_RX_CON24_LN0_MUX | SENINF_MIPI_RX_CON24_LN1_MUX |
@@ -1142,8 +1224,14 @@
 		inst_update(cam, SENINF_CTRL_EXT, SENINF_CTRL_EXT_NCSI2_IP_EN, 0);
 	}
 
-	/* Plain RAW10, no DPCM compression. */
-	writel(SENINF_CSI2_DPCM_DI_30_EN, inst + SENINF_CSI2_DPCM);
+	/*
+	 * Plain RAW10, no DPCM compression -- so leave the DPCM block alone.
+	 * The streaming vendor reads 0 here; we used to set DI_30_EN, which
+	 * asks the decoder to expand data type 0x30 that this sensor never
+	 * sends. Harmless on its own (measured), but there is no reason to
+	 * differ from the vendor on a register this driver does not need.
+	 */
+	writel(0, inst + SENINF_CSI2_DPCM);
 
 	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CSI2_LNRD_TIMING,
 		    SENINF_CSI2_LNRD_TIMING_DATA_SETTLE, settle_delay);
@@ -1158,8 +1246,16 @@
 		    SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_BYPASS, 0);
 	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_CTL,
 		    SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_CDPHY_SEL, 0);
+	/*
+	 * 1, not the 3 this used to write: the streaming vendor's whole
+	 * register reads 0x01. gemini_cam_rx_config() runs after this and sets
+	 * the same field, so the two have to agree or the readback lies about
+	 * which line configured it.
+	 */
+	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_CTL,
+		    SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_CPHY_CNT, 1);
 	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_CTL,
-		    SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_CPHY_CNT, 3);
+		    SENINF_CSI2_RESYNC_MERGE_EN, 0);
 	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CSI2_MODE, SENINF_CSI2_MODE_CSI2_MODE, 0);
 	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CSI2_MODE, SENINF_CSI2_MODE_HEADER_LEN, 0);
 	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CSI2_DPHY_SYNC, SENINF_CSI2_DPHY_SYNC_MASK_0,
@@ -1168,7 +1264,8 @@
 		    0x001d);
 
 	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CSI2_CTL, SENINF_CSI2_CTL_CLOCK_HS_OPTION, 0);
-	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CSI2_CTL, SENINF_CSI2_CTL_HSRX_DET_EN, 0);
+	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CSI2_CTL, SENINF_CSI2_CTL_HSRX_DET_EN,
+		    hsrx_det ? 1 : 0);
 	if (csi2_ctl_or)
 		writel(readl(inst + SENINF_CSI2_CTL) | csi2_ctl_or,
 		       inst + SENINF_CSI2_CTL);
@@ -1216,13 +1313,25 @@
 	inst_update(cam, SENINF_CTRL, SENINF_CTRL_CSI2_SW_RST, 0);
 
 	/* FIFO mux: one pixel per clock, normal (non-JPEG) FIFO thresholds. */
+	if (phy_late)
+		gemini_cam_phy_setup(cam);
+
+	if (decoder_reset) {
+		/*
+		 * Re-latch the receiver against a now-settled configuration.
+		 * Drop SENINF_EN, let the block see a clean edge, bring it
+		 * back. If the lane FSM was stuck from being enabled while the
+		 * analog was already toggling, this is what would free it.
+		 */
+		inst_update(cam, SENINF_CTRL, SENINF_CTRL_SENINF_EN, 0);
+		udelay(20);
+		inst_update(cam, SENINF_CTRL, SENINF_CTRL_SENINF_EN, 1);
+		udelay(20);
+	}
+
 	mux_update(cam, SENINF_MUX_CTRL, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_MUX_EN, 1);
 	mux_update(cam, SENINF_MUX_CTRL, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_SRC_SEL,
 		   SENINF_MIPI_SENSOR);
-	mux_update(cam, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_EXT, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_EXT_SRC_SEL_EXT,
-		   SENINF_NORMAL_MODEL);
-	mux_update(cam, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_EXT, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_EXT_PIX_SEL_EXT,
-		   pixel_mode == 4 ? 1 : 0);
 	mux_update(cam, SENINF_MUX_CTRL, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_PIX_SEL,
 		   pixel_mode == 2 ? 1 : 0);
 	mux_update(cam, SENINF_MUX_CTRL, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_FIFO_FULL_WR_EN, 2);
@@ -1239,6 +1348,22 @@
 	writel(val & ~(SENINF_MUX_CTRL_IRQ_SW_RST | SENINF_MUX_CTRL_MUX_SW_RST),
 	       mux + SENINF_MUX_CTRL);
 
+	/*
+	 * MUX_CTRL_EXT is programmed HERE, after the mux software reset, and
+	 * not before it: the reset clears the register, and for as long as
+	 * these two writes were the other way round SRC_SEL_EXT read back 0
+	 * against the vendor's 1 and the FIFO mux ignored the stream
+	 * completely -- MUX_INTSTA 0, no frame size, nothing reaching CAMSV,
+	 * with a CSI-2 decoder that was by then working perfectly. Setting it
+	 * mid-stream by hand is what proved it (2026-08-26): MUX_INTSTA went
+	 * to the vendor's 0x58 and the mux began measuring frames in the same
+	 * second.
+	 */
+	mux_update(cam, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_EXT, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_EXT_SRC_SEL_EXT,
+		   SENINF_NORMAL_MODEL);
+	mux_update(cam, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_EXT, SENINF_MUX_CTRL_EXT_PIX_SEL_EXT,
+		   pixel_mode == 4 ? 1 : 0);
+
 	writel(SENINF_MUX_SPARE_FIFO_FULL_SEL | 0xc0000, mux + SENINF_MUX_SPARE);
 
 	/* Report every mux error the block can raise, so overflows are counted. */
